.TH TAG 1 "October 2026" "finder-tags 9.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tag \- manipulate macOS Finder tags while preserving tag order
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tag
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B tag --add
.I TAGS
.RI [ placement ]
.I path ...
.br
.B tag --remove
.I TAGS path ...
.br
.B tag --set
.I TAGS path ...
.br
.B tag --fix
.I path ...
.br
.B tag --match
.I TAGS
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B tag --filter
.I TAGS
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B tag --usage
.I TAGS
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B tag --find
.I TAGS
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B tag --copy
.I SOURCE DESTINATION
.br
.B tag --export
.RI [ ROOT ]
.br
.B tag --restore
.I ARCHIVE
.RI [ --root DEST ]
.br
.B tag --convert
.I ARCHIVE
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B tag --move
.I TAG POSITION path ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B finder-tags
installs an executable named
.B tag
for listing and modifying Finder tags. Stored tag order is preserved by default.
The interface is compatible with jdberry/tag where practical.
.SH OPERATIONS
.TP
.BR -l , " --list"
List tags. This is the default operation.
.TP
.B --export [ROOT]
Write a versioned, root-relative JSONL archive for one directory. Hidden and
untagged items are included by default. File information is included by
default and can be disabled with --no-file-info. Each item's colors array
stores one color number per tag; null explicitly restores a missing color suffix.
Export archives require a final summary record. Summary counts and exporter
error totals are not yet validated.
.TP
.B --restore ARCHIVE
Restore an archive from a file, or from standard input when ARCHIVE is -. Use
--root DEST to relocate the archive's one root. Restore requires the same
--follow-symlinks setting recorded in the archive. With -L, redirected followed
targets produce warnings.
Without -L, paths through parent symlinks are rejected before writing and
checked again before each item. Entries are not locked against concurrent replacement.
Tag I/O for a listed symlink itself uses its referent, as in ordinary commands.
The final summary distinguishes restored or would-change items, cleared or
would-clear tags, unchanged items, missing items, skipped items, errors, and
warnings. Missing and skipped items are also reported by path.
.TP
.B --convert ARCHIVE
Read a JSONL archive from a file or standard input and print human-readable
output. Display options such as --reverse, --slash, --space-indent,
--align-tags, and --color apply only to the converted output; the output is not
restoreable. --jsonl and --ndjson are rejected with this text-only operation.
.TP
.BR -a , " --add " TAGS
Add or re-case tags without disturbing unrelated tag order.
.TP
.B --append TAGS
Alias for --add with last/right/top placement.
.TP
.B --prepend TAGS
Alias for --add with first/left/bottom placement.
.TP
.BR -r , " --remove " TAGS
Remove matching tags. A TAGS value of * removes all tags.
--del and --delete are synonyms for --remove.
.TP
.BR -s , " --set " TAGS
Replace the complete tag array.
--replace is a synonym for --set.
.TP
.B --fix
Repair invalid tag names and remove empty tags. Valid tags keep their order
and colors. --repair is a synonym for --fix.
.TP
.B --copy SOURCE DESTINATION
Replace DESTINATION's ordered tag names and stored colors with SOURCE's tags.
Color differences are copied even when the names already match.
.TP
.BR -m , " --match " TAGS
List traversed files matching TAGS. --match and --filter use the same matching
expression; --match hides tags by default.
.TP
.B --filter TAGS
List traversed files matching TAGS using list-style output, which shows tags by
default. Query expressions use comma for AND, | for OR, and a leading - for
NOT. Precedence is comma < | < -; parentheses override it. For example,
--filter 'Project,Red|Orange' matches Project and either Red or Orange. Escape
a pipe in an unquoted tag as \\|. The * term requires at least one tag and -*
requires no tags.
.TP
.BR -u , " --usage " TAGS
Count all tags on traversed files matching TAGS.
.TP
.BR -f , " --find " TAGS
Use Spotlight to find matching files, optionally scoped to supplied paths.
.TP
.B --move TAG POSITION
Move one tag to a new position.
.SH ORDERING
.TP
.B --at POSITION
Insertion position for --add or --move. POSITION may be a zero-based index,
first/left/bottom, or last/right/top.
.TP
.B --before TAG
Place added or moved tags immediately before TAG.
.TP
.B --after TAG
Place added or moved tags immediately after TAG.
.TP
.B --sorted-tags
Sort tags for display. Mutating operations also sort the final stored array.
Read-only operations never write merely because this option is present.
.TP
.BR -V , " --reverse"
Reverse display order. Export ignores this option so JSONL archives always
store Foundation's natural tag order.
.TP
.BR -C , " --case-sensitive"
Use exact-case matching instead of the default case-insensitive matching.
.SH OUTPUT
Normal text listings, previews, and diagnostics show control characters as
visible escapes such as \en, \et, and \eu{1b} for ESC. Unicode line and
direction controls are escaped; ordinary Unicode and emoji are preserved.
Literal backslashes are doubled. Escaping applies to redirected text too,
before the program adds colors and layout. Stored names, JSON values, and
NUL-delimited stdout retain their original contents. Diagnostics are always
escaped. Use -0 --no-tags for an exact filename stream.
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Show brief help. Use -hh or -h -v for detailed help, including all options,
aliases, examples, and behavior notes. Help and verbosity flags may appear in
either order; --help --verbose is equivalent.
.TP
.BR -q , " --quiet"
Decrease verbosity by one. Suppress routine text summaries, retaining warnings,
errors, backup paths, dry-run previews, and requested listing/JSONL output.
.TP
.BR -v , " --verbose"
Increase verbosity by one. Show informational messages on standard error.
Mutations include their before/after tag names and color numbers; Spotlight searches report that indexed
results determine membership. Usage reports conflicting stored color numbers.
With --help, show detailed help.
.PP
Repeated -q and -v accumulate and cancel one for one: -qqv is quiet,
-qv is default, and -vvq is verbose. Levels below default are quiet;
levels above default are verbose.
.TP
.B --version
Show the program version.
.TP
.BR -c , " --color[=yes/no]"
Display each tag's stored color when stdout is a terminal. Names and Finder
preferences do not determine displayed colors. Zero, unavailable, and unknown
color numbers are displayed uncolored.
Explicit settings use =: auto detects terminals; yes/on/always/force always
use color; no/off/none/never disable color. Bare --color and -c use auto.
JSONL is never colored. Error and warning prefixes use bold red; automatic
mode checks whether stderr is a terminal for diagnostics.
.TP
.B --no-color
Alias for --color=off. The last explicit color setting wins.
.TP
.BR -n , " --filename"
Show filenames. --name is an alias.
.TP
.BR -N , " --no-filename"
Hide filenames. --no-name is an alias.
.TP
.BR -t , " --tags"
Show tags.
.TP
.BR -T , " --no-tags"
Hide tags.
.TP
.BR -g , " --one-per-line"
Display each tag on its own line. --garrulous is an alias.
.TP
.BR -G , " --comma-separated"
Display comma-separated tags. --no-garrulous is an alias.
.TP
.B --space-indent
Use two spaces instead of a tab before tags in list, match, find, usage, or
converted output.
.TP
.B --align-tags N
In text file-list output, right-pad each file path to at least N display columns,
then use two spaces before tags. This implies --space-indent and is not valid
with JSONL output. Common full-width and emoji characters count as two columns.
.TP
.B --compact-tags
Mimic Finder's mini color-circles in text file-list output by displaying the
first character of each tag in reverse tag order, with no separating commas.
This implies --color and --reverse and is not valid with JSONL output. Without
--space-indent, one- and two-tag outputs receive extra spaces so their initials
align with three-tag output; --align-tags includes those spaces in its per-file
minimum width.
.TP
.BR -p , " --slash"
Append / to directory names and @ to symlink names.
.TP
.B --print-symlinks
Print each symlink's stored destination. A directory target receives a
trailing / when --slash is also specified. Missing targets add [BROKEN SYMLINK]
to the filename, bold with a red background when color is enabled.
.TP
.BR -0 , " --null"
Terminate text records with NUL and leave names unescaped. --nul is an alias.
Broken-symlink markers are omitted to keep filenames literal.
.TP
.B --absolute
Display absolute logical paths.
.TP
.B --jsonl
Emit newline-delimited JSON. --ndjson is an alias. Item paths are relative to
the current root record, with . for the root item itself, unless --absolute
is selected. Spotlight results and copy source paths are absolute.
An unavailable item type produces a warning and an omitted kind field;
readable tags are retained. Export and backup require a known item type.
.TP
.B --tagged-only
Process only items having at least one tag. With --copy, it filters the source
file.
.TP
.B --file-info
Include each listed, matched, found, or exported item's byte size and content
modification time. Export enables this by default. JSONL uses numeric size and mtime fields;
restore ignores these fields. For an existing symlink, size and mtime come
from its target even without -L, matching Finder's usual tag lookup; -L still
controls traversal and structural archive symlink metadata. Missing targets
use the symlink's own timestamp, size, and tags, with one final warning count.
Text output shows
file sizes below 0.05 MB as ~0MB, sizes from 0.05 MB to below 0.95 MB rounded
to one decimal without a leading zero (for example, .5MB), and sizes of at
least 0.95 MB rounded to the nearest whole MB. MB uses decimal units
(1 MB = 1,000,000 bytes). Directories are shown as ---.
Unavailable size or modification time produces a warning and [--------    ---]
in text output; JSONL omits the unavailable fields.
Permission errors and symlink loops are reported as unavailable target
information, not missing targets. Unknown targetExists and targetKind fields
are omitted while the known link destination is retained.
.TP
.B --no-file-info
Omit size and modification-time fields from export output.
.SH PATH INPUT AND TRAVERSAL
By default, filesystem enumeration sorts each directory's children in
case-sensitive, locale-independent filename order (file10 precedes file2).
Recursive traversal is depth-first: each directory is processed before its
contents, then traversal continues with the next sibling. --sorted-tags and
--reverse affect tag order only.
.PP
Explicit path operands retain their argument order. Glob patterns are expanded
and ordered by the shell before tag receives them. Stdin paths follow
command-line paths and retain their input order. Spotlight --find results are
sorted by display name in ascending order unless --no-sort-files is used.
.TP
.B --stdin
Read LF- or CRLF-delimited paths from standard input.
Only CR immediately before LF is removed; other CR characters belong to the path.
Invalid UTF-8 or a NUL inside a line rejects the entire input before any files
are processed, including command-line paths.
.TP
.B --stdin0
Read NUL-delimited paths from standard input.
Preserve CR/LF characters within paths. Invalid UTF-8 rejects the entire input
before any files are processed.
.TP
.BR -A , " --all" , " --dot-files"
Include hidden files and directories while enumerating, including during
recursive traversal. --dot-files and --include-hidden are aliases for --all.
Hidden entries include
dot-prefixed names and other entries marked hidden by macOS; they are skipped
by default. Explicitly supplied hidden paths are processed regardless of this
option. Export includes hidden entries by default.
.TP
.BR -e , " --enter"
Enumerate immediate contents of explicit directories.
.TP
.BR -R , " -d ," " --recursive"
Recursively enumerate directories.
.TP
.B --no-sort-files
Skip filename sorting during filesystem traversal, including export, and
disable display-name sorting for Spotlight --find. Filesystem and unsorted
Spotlight result order are unspecified; no filename, creation-date,
modification-date, or Finder display order is guaranteed. Recursion remains
depth-first. Explicit arguments, shell globs, and stdin paths retain their
input order. Valid with filesystem traversal operations and Spotlight --find.
.TP
.B --resolve-symlinks0
Resolve symlinks supplied as path operands to their targets when possible,
like find -H. Symlinks encountered during traversal are not followed by this
option alone.
.TP
.B --no-follow-symlinks
Do not recursively follow symlinked directories. Explicit operand resolution is
controlled separately by --resolve-symlinks0. This is the default.
.TP
.B -L, --follow-symlinks
Follow symlinks and symlinked directories. Archive items may record structural
target information, but target tags are never nested inside symlink metadata.
.PP
Filesystem inspection errors are reported and skip the affected traversal or
restore work. Restore distinguishes inaccessible items from missing items.
.TP
.B --exclude PATH
Skip PATH and its descendants during filesystem traversal. The option may be
repeated. A single component, such as .git or .git/, matches that component at
any depth; a path containing / is interpreted relative to each traversal root.
The normalized exclusions are recorded in an export archive header. This option
does not affect restore or Spotlight --find.
.SH SAFETY
.TP
.B --dry-run
Compute and display intended mutations without writing. Restore reports the
same change count as a real run. --dryrun is an alias.
.TP
.B --backup
Enable a temporary per-file undo archive before mutations. Backup defaults to off
for explicit command-line paths and to on for the entire operation with
-R/--recursive, -e/--enter, stdin path input, or --restore.
-R enables this default even when all supplied paths are files; recursive
aliases use the same rule. Default archives use the system temporary directory.
Explicit backup options override the default; the last one wins.
.TP
.B --backup-path PATH
Enable a per-file undo archive at PATH before mutations.
Existing files and symlinks at PATH are refused using exclusive creation.
Creation occurs before the first tag write; failure stops the command with an
error. A later backup record write or sync failure stops before the pending edit,
without rolling back earlier edits. Backup errors suggest --no-backup to run
without backup.
Undo journals record resolved file paths, allow repeated paths, replay in reverse
mutation order, and do not require a final summary.
.TP
.B --no-backup
Disable the undo archive.
.TP
.B --sync-backup
Sync each undo record before its mutation when backup is enabled. This is more
durable but can be expensive.
.SH TAG SYNTAX
Mutation TAGS is comma separated. Quoted CSV-style components permit literal
commas, for example:
.PP
.nf
tag --set 'Red,"Project, Alpha","Needs review"' file
.fi
.PP
Matching is case-insensitive by default while stored spelling is preserved.
Query TAGS for --match, --filter, --usage, and --find use the same expression
grammar: comma means AND, | means OR, a leading - means NOT, and parentheses
override the precedence comma < | < -. Quote operator characters inside a tag,
or escape a pipe in an unquoted tag as \\|.
Each CR, LF, or NUL in a supplied or stored tag name is replaced with the visible
replacement character (U+FFFD), with a warning showing the repair. CRLF becomes
two replacement characters. Empty tag-list entries are ignored. Non-string
stored entries become text tags with warnings. An invalid stored color suffix
is preserved as part of the repaired name with color unavailable. Unreadable
or non-array tag metadata still reports an error. Reads and dry runs do not
rewrite metadata; subsequent tag changes and undo records use recovered tags.
.SH FILES
See the finder-tags README and REVIEW.md for detailed behavior, reliability
notes, compatibility differences, archive syntax, and JSONL fields.
